    {"id": "patch_f98cd079ac46", "failure_port": "o_mosi", "source_line": 9789, "mechanism": "When r_use_fifo=1 and r_fifo_wr=1 during a write command, the original code incorrectly enters FIFO read mode (r_cmd_state=6) because it only checks r_use_fifo. The patch adds (!r_fifo_wr) guard so r_cmd_state advances to state 7 (FIFO write) instead of state 6 (FIFO read). This prevents mis-sequencing MOSI bytes (0xFE start token vs data bytes), fixing early o_mosi=1 at cycle 484126 and subsequent pattern mismatches."},
    {"id": "patch_aecf7561e2c6", "failure_port": "o_mosi", "source_line": 9789, "mechanism": "During FIFO write (r_use_fifo=1, r_fifo_wr=1), r_have_resp becomes true once the card responds. The original then sets ll_fifo_rd=1 and ll_cmd_dat=fifo_byte because it only checks r_fifo_wr. The patch adds (!r_fifo_wr) to the ll_fifo_rd assignment, keeping ll_fifo_rd=0 during writes so MOSI continues sending 0xFF/0xFE instead of reading FIFO data, correcting o_mosi sequence from cycle 484126 onward."},
    {"id": "patch_9f849ba2dadb", "failure_port": "o_mosi", "source_line": 9994, "mechanism": "In SDSPI_RSP_WRITING state (r_rsp_state=7), the response handler waits for ll_fifo_wr_complete or r_read_err_token[0]. If r_have_resp stays asserted from a previous read, the state machine may not transition to RDCOMPLETE. Adding (!r_have_resp) ensures the write path clears cleanly, preventing stale response flags from corrupting MOSI timing."},
    {"id": "patch_1fa9da7ac917", "failure_port": "o_mosi", "source_line": 9734, "mechanism": "In command state 5 (waiting for response), the original advances based on r_have_resp alone. If r_have_resp is set spuriously, the FIFO path (state 6/7) is entered with wrong ll_fifo_rd/ll_fifo_wr. Qualifying with (!r_have_resp) would be inverted logic; this candidate likely needs (r_have_resp) instead. Kept low rank as the guard direction appears opposite to required fix."},
    {"id": "patch_3fe26dfae867", "failure_port": "o_mosi", "source_line": 9734, "mechanism": "Opposite polarity to patch_1fa9da7ac917: adds (r_have_resp) guard to the same condition. This correctly ensures state advance only when response is actually received, preventing premature FIFO state entry that would desynchronize MOSI byte emission (first mismatch at cycle 484126)."},
    {"id": "patch_70dca1511670", "failure_port": "o_mosi", "source_line": 10066, "mechanism": "In SDSPI_RSP_WRITING, adds (r_fifo_wr) to the completion condition alongside ll_fifo_wr_complete. Ensures write path only terminates during actual FIFO write operations, not during reads or idle, preventing early ll_cmd_stb deassertion that would truncate MOSI stream."},
    {"id": "patch_bdc33e6d7cdf", "failure_port": "o_mosi", "source_line": 9820, "mechanism": "When sending CMD55/ACMD23 argument (0xFE start token) during FIFO write, the original sets ll_fifo_wr=1 unconditionally on r_use_fifo. The patch adds (!r_fifo_wr) so FIFO write enable only asserts for actual data phase, not the start-token cycle. Prevents off-by-one FIFO address advance corrupting subsequent MOSI data."},
    {"id": "patch_0ce0a636b1ee", "failure_port": "o_mosi", "source_line": 9994, "mechanism": "In SDSPI_RSP_GETTOKEN handling, adds (ll_fifo_rd_complete) to the token-wait condition. Ensures FIFO read completion is confirmed before transitioning to WAIT_WHILE_BUSY, preventing premature state advance that would misalign MOSI byte boundaries during multi-block reads."}
  ],
  "explanation": "All top candidates target the command/response state machines (sdspi.v lines 9734-10066) that drive ll_cmd_stb, ll_fifo_rd, ll_fifo_wr and ll_cmd_dat — the direct inputs to llsdspi which generates o_mosi. The first mismatch at cycle 484126 (o_mosi=1 vs expected 0) occurs during a multi-block CMD18 read with FIFO enabled. Patches 1-2 fix the core write/read path confusion in r_cmd_state 5→6/7 transition. Patches 3,6,7 harden completion conditions in SDSPI_RSP_WRITING/GETTOKEN. Patch 5 corrects the polarity of the r_have_resp guard. Byte-order patches (03 lower-ranked condition gates address secondary token/CRC paths less likely to cause the initial MOSI divergence."
}
